[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Today, I\'m going to show you how to generate long AI videos with multiple shots and full control over every single moment using [music] LTX director, which is a completely free timeline editor inside ComfyUI. Using this, you can add images, [music] text prompts, and even'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audio segments to control exactly what happens and when. And in my opinion, this is the best thing that happened to open-source AI video models in a while. So, let\'s get right into it.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First thing you need to do is install ComfyUI. You can download it from comfy.org. It\'s super easy to set up. If this is your first time using it though, I\'ve'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got another video that walks through it in way more detail. So, go watch that first if you need to. Once that\'s done, launch ComfyUI. Now, before you go any further, I\'ve put a link to a full guide in the description that you will want to'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'follow to get all the models and extensions the workflow needs. Do not skip it or nothing is going to work. Download the LTX director workflow from the description box and just drag and'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drop the JSON file into ComfyUI to open it. And hey, if all that sounds like too much work or you run into any issues along the way, I also linked the same workflow on RunComfy where you can use'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the whole thing online with access to high-end GPUs. That way, you can skip the installation altogether, but the process is exactly the same, so you can still follow this video. All right, everything is set up now. So, let\'s try'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a simple image-to-video sequence. I\'ve got these individual frames I want to turn into one coherent sequence. I\'ll just drag and drop the first image into the timeline here. You can adjust the duration of the shot. Then, let\'s type'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the video prompt in this box. This workflow uses LTX 2.3, which works way better with detailed prompts. So, make sure you use actual filmmaking vocabulary for framing, camera movement,'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lighting, and so on. You will notice I also added an audio description since LTX 2.3 can generate sound and dialogue, too. Then there\'s the global prompt box. [music] This applies to the whole'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sequence. It\'s good for keeping the visual style consistent across shots, for example, but it\'s totally optional. You don\'t have to use it. Now, let\'s add a second image right after the first one. I want this one to be around 3'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seconds and [music] since we\'ve already gone past the current video duration, and I\'ve got more images to add, I\'ll just extend it. Let\'s say 9 seconds for now. And here is the third image already added. I also'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to emphasize that your input images matter just as much as the prompt. The visual style and consistent characters across your frames is what really sells the final sequence. And by the way, I'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generated every one of the frames you saw and will see in this video inside Open Art. Open Art is an all-in-one platform that gives you access to over 100 of the best image and video models in one place. Nanobanana Pro, GPT Image'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2, [music] Kling, Cedance 2.0, and more. Basically, every AI tool you need without jumping between multiple websites. They also recently launched their newest big feature, Open Art'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Director, which lets you generate a full video from just a prompt. You describe the story and characters, and it handles everything. Visuals, voice, music, you even get a timeline editor. People are'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using this to generate product ads, [music] music videos, and even short films. Open Art is used by over 8 million people [music] every single month, and I\'ve genuinely been relying'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on it for years now. Link is in the description if you want to check it out, and make sure you use code MDMZ20 [music] for 20% off any monthly plan. Back to LTX Director, right now the custom width and height are set to zero, which means'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it will just follow your input images [music] dimensions. And that\'s a bad idea if your image is too high res, because the higher the resolution, the longer it [music] takes to generate, and every machine has its limits. So, at'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'least set the width and it will auto set the height based on your input\'s aspect [music] ratio. I usually go with 1024 or 1280. Yeah, the output quality might look a bit soft at that size, but you'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can just run the generated video through an upscaler afterward. My go-to right now is Topaz Video. It delivers insane results. I\'ll link it in the description. There are also free alternatives like C [music] DVR 2.5 that'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works right inside ComfyUI. And I\'ve actually got a dedicated tutorial on that if you want to check it out. Back in LTX director, we have now got a sequence of three clips [music] ready to'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go. So, let\'s hit run. On my machine, this took about 3 minutes. And as you can see, I\'ve got a sequence where each clip plays out exactly how I directed it in the prompt.'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Honestly, this is a really solid result for an open-source model. I\'ll be honest though, it took me a couple tries to get here. My biggest mistake early on was'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'misjudging the duration of each clip. You need to leave enough time for whatever action you\'re describing to actually happen. Once you\'ve got a sequence that\'s working, you can just keep building on it, adding more clips'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as you go. One thing I really wanted in the first clip of this sequence is for the headlights to turn on before the car drives off. Now, sure, I could have just mentioned that in the prompt, but I'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'still wouldn\'t have control over exactly when it happens. That\'s where prompt relay comes in. So, first, I will shorten the first clip and push everything else further down the'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'timeline to leave room for new events. I\'ll change this prompt so that for the first 2 seconds the car stays completely [music] still. Then I\'m going to click here to add a new text segment. I want'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to break this down even more, so I will shorten this one to 2 seconds and focus the prompt entirely on the headlights switching on. Now I will add one more text segment [music] to fill the gap and'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is where I want the car to actually drive off before we move into the rest of the sequence. Since I\'m just testing this part, I will leave the last two shots out for now and hit run. And there it is. The headlights turn on'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly when I want it. Here\'s another example where I directed a whole series of precise actions before the character delivers a line. First he raises the cigarette to his lips and takes a drag,'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then lowers it back down, exhales some smoke, takes off his sunglasses with both hands, and finally looking straight at the camera he says, &gt;&gt; You picked the wrong street, my friend.'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Action by action, word for word, and all from a single image input. By the way, if you\'ve got any questions so far about settings, prompts, anything, drop it in the comments. I\'ll be happy to help. And'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you\'re new here, I make a lot of AI, visual effects, and filmmaking tutorials. So if that\'s the kind of thing you\'re into, please subscribe [music] and enable notifications so you won\'t miss any of my new videos. [music]'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now LTX Director is not just about controlling what happens after a frame. You can also direct what happens before it. For example, here I wanted the runner to start [music] standing up'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first, then lowers down into a crouch. I simply added a text segment before [music] the image. Although it\'s a simple standing pose, I made sure to describe it in detail and really direct the transition between the two states.'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And at the same time, I added another segment after the image. This is where I want the runner to take off at speed. Sure enough, that\'s exactly what I got in the output. It imagined [music] a completely new framing, filled in the'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gaps between my segments, and followed the prompts precisely. Here\'s another example where I directed multiple actions before [music] the image using Prompt Relay. It starts with just the background, then the man walks'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into frame, lifts his arm, and says, &gt;&gt; You should have stayed home. &gt;&gt; and then fires his gun. You can also use LT\'s Director to generate one-shot sequences, no cuts at all.'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This works really well if you\'ve got multiple frames from a single scene and the environment doesn\'t really change that much. The trick is for every image, describe what leads into the next frame.'

[08:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For example, here I described her eyelids snapping open, revealing the glowing blue iris underneath, which is exactly what shows up in the next frame. Same idea here, I wrote the camera zooms'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out, revealing the female character, and that\'s exactly what plays out next. I\'ll be honest, I got way more failed generations trying to nail one-shot sequences than anything else in this'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video, but when it works, it\'s [music] genuinely impressive. Now, you\'ve seen in the earlier examples that LT\'s Director can generate speech on its own, and it actually sounds pretty good. But what if you\'ve got multiple characters'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talking throughout the sequence and you want more control over what they say, how they say it, and most importantly, you want a consistent voice for each character. The good news is that you can also upload your own audio files and use'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that to drive the generation. So, here I\'m building a sequence where two characters are having a conversation. I used Eleven Labs to pre-generate their dialogue, then just added those audio files to the audio track in sync with'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the frames. &gt;&gt; What took you so long? You know I don\'t like waiting. &gt;&gt; It\'s important to still add a text prompt to each image, too, describe their expression. And if you\'ve got'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multiple characters in the same shot, you can just tell the AI which one is speaking. Like here, I mentioned the bearded man on the right. But here\'s the important part. Make sure you follow that with the exact same line spoken in'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the audio file. I also recommend leaving a small gap after each audio file. If you skip that, you will likely get weird results, characters not saying anything, or even saying someone else\'s line.'

[10:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Besides using custom audio to drive the generation, you can also add body movement or actions for your characters. For example, here I added the character lifts her gloved hand and takes off her'

[10:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sunglasses as she exhales and says. I also added a global prompt pushing for better lip sync and more lively facial expressions and hand gestures just to bring the scene to life a bit more.'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; What took you so long? You know I don\'t like waiting. &gt;&gt; [sighs] &gt;&gt; I had to make sure nobody followed me here. &gt;&gt; Let\'s not waste any more time. Tell me about what you saw out there.'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Another powerful use case of LTX director is extending existing videos. You can literally import video clips into the timeline just like we did with images. For example, here I\'ve got a 2-second clip. Some tension is building,'

[11:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but nothing much actually happens yet. I want the character to get up and express some emotion. So I just add text segments after the video and describe a series of actions. The man stands up'

[11:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'abruptly, then shouts something in anger. Let\'s see what I got. &gt;&gt; GET OUT OF MY HOUSE! &gt;&gt; I WANTED to push it further and'

[11:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experiment with some visual effects. And while the principle\'s there, and it does follow my prompts, I think it still has some limitations being a free, open-source model, especially with more complex physics. That said, I will keep'

[11:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'testing to see how far I can push it. Quick shout-out to the original creator of this workflow, What Dreams Cost, who has uploaded a bunch of videos on his own channel showing off more examples'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and use [music] cases of LT LX Director. Definitely worth checking out. I\'m genuinely excited to see what he comes up with next. If you enjoyed this video, please give it a like. It seriously helps out the channel more than you can'

[12:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'imagine. Stay creative, and I\'ll catch you in the next one. Peace. &gt;&gt; [music]'
